--- Finding (blossom_algorithm): a variable may be named like a word the parser
--- teaches against (`match`, `from`, `print`, …: parser.rs python_keyword_hint)
--- — `match = [1, 2]` binds and `log[match]` reads it — but an interpolation
--- that begins with the name is refused, in both modes, AT LINE 1, COLUMN 1 of
--- the file (the interpolation's own position, not the file's):
---
---   t.co:1:1: Crow removed 'match' — use if / else. There are no sum types or
---   pattern matching (see Design Note: No Pattern Matching).
---     1 │ --- Finding (blossom_algorithm): …
---
--- Expected: [1, 2] then 1 — or, if the name is not to be used, a refusal at
--- the binding, as a core name's is. The hint fires because the
--- interpolation is parsed as a statement whose first token is the name and is
--- not followed by `=` (parser.rs parse_stmt). The same rule refuses an index
--- write at statement start, with the right position this time:
--- `match[0] = 5` → t.co:4:1: Crow removed 'match' …; and `from = 3` then
--- `log['`from`']` → t.co:1:1: Crow imports with 'use module' …, not 'import'.
--- `log['`print`']` alone → t.co:1:1: Crow prints with 'log[value]' ….
match = [1, 2]
log[match]
log['`match[0]`']
